'''Lidia Bikarí''' is a Guajicutean politician of the [[Partido Popular Guaji]] and the principal modern political figure associated with the late-2010s Bikuismo revival. She became known for arguing that Bikumaku should return to public life not only as a historical script but as part of everyday state visibility.

== Name ==

Bikarí's surname derives from ''bika'', the Guaji root for a carved mark or inscription, combined with ''-rí'', a suffix indicating a person of a lineage or place. The name therefore carries the sense of "person of the carved mark", a fitting form for a politician associated with script revival.

== Political career ==

Bikarí emerged in Guajicutean politics in the late 2010s, when language and cultural questions began to draw more public attention again. Working through the [[Partido Popular Guaji]], she tied script visibility to national identity, public education, and the everyday presence of Guaji culture in cities and towns.

Her campaign drew support from teachers, artists, rural cultural activists, and younger urban Guaji voters. Rather than presenting Bikumaku as a museum object, she framed it as a living civic resource that should appear in public spaces alongside the Mesogean script.

== Bikuismo revival ==

Bikarí became the leading political face of the modern Bikuismo revival. The movement widened interest in the old script across posters, public design, school materials, and municipal campaigns, and it helped build support for the [[Bikumaku Language Act]] of 2021.

The revival she promoted was distinct from the 19th-century artistic Bikuismo movement, but it drew on the same visual language and historical symbolism. Where the earlier movement had treated Bikumaku as a cultural archive, Bikarí's campaign treated it as a policy issue as well.

